WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:05.020
Just like at the counter of Los Pollos Hermanos,
there's a whole lot going on behind the scenes

00:00:05.020 --> 00:00:05.820
of Breaking Bad.

00:00:06.150 --> 00:00:10.840
From shocking character choices to the show's
lasting legacy, here's the stuff that only

00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:13.100
true fans know about the award-winning show.

00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:19.720
Once primarily a repository for old movies,
AMC launched Mad Men in 2007 and Breaking

00:00:19.730 --> 00:00:25.060
Bad in 2008, positioning itself as a major
"peak TV" player alongside the likes of TNT,

00:00:25.060 --> 00:00:26.660
HBO, and FX.

00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:31.700
Oddly enough, all those networks had the chance
to take on Breaking Bad and turned it down.

00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:37.160
As creator Vince Gilligan told the Television
Academy Foundation about his TNT experience:

00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:39.040
"Great meeting at TNT.

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:40.040
Excellent meeting.

00:00:40.040 --> 00:00:43.120
The two executives who I pitched it to were
on the edge of their seat, they were loving

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:43.620
it."

00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:48.960
However, at the end of his breathless description
of the first episode, the executives final

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:52.460
response wasn't nearly as positive as Gilligan
had hoped:

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:56.800
"And then they look at each other, when I
finished, I get 'The End,' and they look to

00:00:56.800 --> 00:01:00.800
each other and they say, 'Oh, God, I wish
we could buy this.'"

00:01:01.220 --> 00:01:05.960
Evidently, they were scared off by the fact
that Walt cooks meth, so they asked Gilligan

00:01:05.960 --> 00:01:08.060
if he could make him a counterfeiter instead.

00:01:08.380 --> 00:01:13.080
Gilligan rightly thought that making Walt
a counterfeiter wouldn't be the right formula,

00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:14.780
so he moved on to other networks.

00:01:14.900 --> 00:01:19.860
While Gilligan says the HBO executive he met
with wasn't interested, FX actually bought

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:22.840
the series, only to eventually reverse course.

00:01:23.110 --> 00:01:27.780
FX President John Landgraf eventually explained
why they passed on the show while talking

00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:29.660
to KCRW's The Business:

00:01:30.420 --> 00:01:33.720
"So we had three dramas with male antiheroes.

00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:38.780
So, the question was, are we defining FX as
the male antihero network, and is that a big

00:01:38.780 --> 00:01:39.920
enough tent?"

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:46.060
Ultimately, FX decided to branch out and greenlit
the Courtney Cox drama Dirt, instead of going

00:01:46.060 --> 00:01:46.980
with Breaking Bad.

00:01:47.240 --> 00:01:50.620
After all these rejections, things were looking
bad for the show.

00:01:50.860 --> 00:01:56.160
As a last resort, his agent sent it to AMC,
which was looking to expand its original programming

00:01:56.160 --> 00:01:56.660
slate.

00:01:57.080 --> 00:01:58.860
And the rest, as they say, is history.

00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:06.860
In addition to TNT, HBO, and FX, Vince Gilligan
also pitched Breaking Bad to Showtime in 2005.

00:02:07.020 --> 00:02:11.640
Gilligan later recalled to Newsweek being
about five minutes into his presentation when

00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:12.940
the network president quipped:

00:02:13.130 --> 00:02:14.920
"'This sounds a lot like Weeds.'"

00:02:15.160 --> 00:02:20.030
Gilligan hadn't heard of Weeds, so he asked
what that was, and the executive explained

00:02:20.030 --> 00:02:24.250
that it was a brand new Showtime series about
a mild-mannered suburban mom who, facing a

00:02:24.250 --> 00:02:27.040
financial emergency, starts selling drugs.

00:02:27.160 --> 00:02:30.900
That's a premise very similar to Breaking
Bad's story about a mild-mannered suburban

00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:34.300
dad who, facing a financial emergency, starts
cooking meth.

00:02:34.660 --> 00:02:37.940
Two representatives from Sony Television were
there with him, and he asked if they knew

00:02:37.940 --> 00:02:38.600
about Weeds.

00:02:38.980 --> 00:02:43.420
They had, but they told Gilligan that Weeds
and Breaking Bad were completely different.

00:02:43.700 --> 00:02:48.440
Gilligan briefly considered abandoning Breaking
Bad altogether, but ultimately, he trusted

00:02:48.440 --> 00:02:49.480
the Sony reps.

00:02:49.540 --> 00:02:52.340
And it's a good thing that he wasn't familiar
with Weeds.

00:02:52.340 --> 00:02:53.600
As Gilligan told Newsweek:

00:02:53.800 --> 00:02:58.700
"If I had known of Weeds weeks or even days
prior to that meeting, it's likely I wouldn't

00:02:58.700 --> 00:02:59.980
have had the will to go on."

00:03:00.880 --> 00:03:06.180
The final episode of Breaking Bad aired in
2013, wrapping up five seasons in a way that

00:03:06.180 --> 00:03:08.460
gave closure to its main characters.

00:03:08.490 --> 00:03:13.600
As you probably know, the show ends with Walter
White dying among his laboratory equipment

00:03:13.600 --> 00:03:18.620
to the strains of Badfinger's "Baby Blue,"
and with Jesse Pinkman escaping his life as

00:03:18.620 --> 00:03:20.860
an enslaved meth cook for Nazis.

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:25.800
That episode was titled "Felina," which just
so happens to be the name of the idealized

00:03:25.800 --> 00:03:29.460
young woman in the 1959 Marty Robbins song
"El Paso."

00:03:29.820 --> 00:03:33.460
That particular song, a country music classic
that hit number one on Billboard, actually

00:03:33.460 --> 00:03:35.500
makes an appearance in the episode.

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:40.060
The tune concerns an outlaw on the run who
returns to the town where he committed a grievous

00:03:40.060 --> 00:03:40.560
crime.

00:03:40.940 --> 00:03:45.420
Hey, you know who else makes a return to the
place where he committed some serious crimes?

00:03:45.420 --> 00:03:46.520
Walter White of Breaking Bad.

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:52.150
Plus, in two strokes of wordplay genius, "Felina"
is an anagram for "finale," and it can be

00:03:52.150 --> 00:03:56.700
broken down into three chemical symbols: Fe,
Li, and Na.

00:03:57.040 --> 00:04:02.860
Those represent iron, lithium, and sodium…which
just so happen to be vital ingredients in

00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:05.420
blood, meth, and tears.

00:04:05.620 --> 00:04:10.180
In other words, "Felina" is what Breaking
Bad was all about all along.

00:04:10.980 --> 00:04:15.140
While "dramedies" have been enjoying their
moment for a while, Breaking Bad is not that

00:04:15.140 --> 00:04:16.680
kind of genre-buster.

00:04:16.980 --> 00:04:18.760
It was an extremely serious show.

00:04:19.120 --> 00:04:23.280
There's not much humor to mine in the story
of a man who descends into darkness when he

00:04:23.289 --> 00:04:27.710
becomes a drug lord in order to pay for cancer
treatments and prevent financial ruin for

00:04:27.710 --> 00:04:29.240
the family he'll leave behind.

00:04:29.520 --> 00:04:34.270
The closest thing the show had to comic relief
was Dean Norris's work as obnoxious, cocky

00:04:34.270 --> 00:04:36.140
DEA agent Hank Schrader.

00:04:36.380 --> 00:04:40.180
And that all stems from Norris's confusion
over how to play the role at his Breaking

00:04:40.180 --> 00:04:40.680
Bad audition.

00:04:41.200 --> 00:04:45.140
As he said on Conan, he doesn't necessarily
think it's a full drama:

00:04:45.240 --> 00:04:46.620
"You thought it was a comedy."

00:04:46.620 --> 00:04:47.120
"I did.

00:04:47.380 --> 00:04:47.880
It's not?"

00:04:48.460 --> 00:04:49.860
"Well, it's interesting because…"

00:04:49.870 --> 00:04:50.870
"It is kinda!"

00:04:50.870 --> 00:04:51.870
"I can understand why you…"

00:04:51.870 --> 00:04:52.860
"It's a black comedy!"

00:04:53.500 --> 00:04:56.420
A lot of Breaking Bad characters die in horrible
ways.

00:04:56.500 --> 00:05:02.140
Walter White dies, Hank Schrader dies, Gus
Fring dies, and Jane Margolis dies, to name

00:05:02.160 --> 00:05:02.660
a few.

00:05:02.680 --> 00:05:06.980
But plenty of other characters survived the
series while coming very close to dying.

00:05:07.360 --> 00:05:12.160
In fact, creator Vince Gilligan nearly killed
off Jesse Pinkman in the first season, and

00:05:12.580 --> 00:05:16.660
he proposed offing Skyler White, played by
Anna Gunn, near the end of the series.

00:05:17.060 --> 00:05:21.140
He came up with the idea that Skyler would
go with her husband and the Disappearer on

00:05:21.140 --> 00:05:23.980
their hideout trip to New Hampshire after
all hope is lost.

00:05:24.180 --> 00:05:29.000
But while hiding out, Skyler offs herself
rather than continue to be with her monstrous

00:05:29.000 --> 00:05:29.500
husband.

00:05:29.860 --> 00:05:34.059
Ultimately, the idea was dropped because the
writers couldn't figure out a way to bring

00:05:34.060 --> 00:05:38.120
Walt Jr. along, if the entire family was going
to go into hiding.

00:05:38.780 --> 00:05:44.879
As masterfully portrayed by Giancarlo Esposito,
Gus Fring is among the most fascinating villains

00:05:44.880 --> 00:05:46.000
in TV history.

00:05:46.280 --> 00:05:51.319
He runs his meth smuggling business with cruelty,
while never losing his dignity and poise.

00:05:51.520 --> 00:05:56.560
He also takes pains to never let his drug
empire interfere with his successful front,

00:05:56.560 --> 00:05:58.980
the fast food franchise Los Pollos Hermanos.

00:05:59.330 --> 00:06:04.680
Gus' rise and fall is crucial to the series'
arc and Walter White's transformation, but

00:06:04.700 --> 00:06:09.480
he surprisingly wasn't originally part of
Breaking Bad creator Vince Gilligan's plan.

00:06:09.800 --> 00:06:16.119
By early season two, frightening drug kingpin
Tuco Salamanca served as the series' "big

00:06:16.120 --> 00:06:19.080
bad," a factor Gilligan wanted to explore
for a while.

00:06:19.300 --> 00:06:24.620
However, the actor who played him, Raymond
Cruz, had to bow out due to prior commitments.

00:06:24.620 --> 00:06:26.080
Gilligan told Digital Spy:

00:06:26.680 --> 00:06:31.159
"So [Raymond Cruz] became unavailable to us,
and we thought, 'Man, we're never gonna have

00:06:31.159 --> 00:06:35.349
a character as good and interesting as he
was,' but we then thought to ourselves, 'Why

00:06:35.349 --> 00:06:37.339
don't we go in the completely opposite direction?'"

00:06:37.880 --> 00:06:43.560
And that's how they decided to create a villain
who was a "buttoned-down, cold-blooded, soft-spoken

00:06:43.560 --> 00:06:44.480
businessman."

00:06:44.560 --> 00:06:46.180
And thus, you get Gus.

00:06:47.120 --> 00:06:52.280
Setting and filming Breaking Bad around Albuquerque,
New Mexico, gave the show a vast and beautiful

00:06:52.280 --> 00:06:56.240
desert landscape where all sorts of criminal
activities could go on unnoticed.

00:06:56.680 --> 00:07:01.680
But it also presented an "Everytown USA" dynamic
to contrast with the dark journey of Walter

00:07:01.680 --> 00:07:02.180
White.

00:07:02.280 --> 00:07:06.620
It wouldn't have been the same show were it
set somewhere else … and it almost was.

00:07:06.720 --> 00:07:11.180
In the 1990s, Riverside, California, earned
a reputation as one of the United States'

00:07:11.180 --> 00:07:16.060
most active meth turfs, and that's where Vince
Gilligan initially set his series.

00:07:16.320 --> 00:07:21.219
Studio Sony Television suggested to Gilligan
that he change Riverside to Albuquerque because

00:07:21.220 --> 00:07:25.210
the show would be cheaper to shoot there,
owing to tax breaks for TV productions.

00:07:25.560 --> 00:07:27.680
The rest, as they say, is television history.

00:07:28.620 --> 00:07:33.220
Breaking Bad fans love to visit the real-life
locations depicted on the show.

00:07:33.340 --> 00:07:37.500
One of the most sought-after locales is the
Whites' humble home, although the people who

00:07:37.500 --> 00:07:39.880
live in and near the house are over it.

00:07:40.310 --> 00:07:45.280
The residents of the White house got so tired
of gawkers, and fans who stole rocks and other

00:07:45.280 --> 00:07:50.420
garden items as souvenirs, that they ordered
construction of a six-foot-tall iron fence.

00:07:50.660 --> 00:07:54.440
The homeowner's daughter told Albuquerque's
KOB that the fans:

00:07:54.720 --> 00:07:58.999
"They feel the need to tell us to close our
garage, get out of the picture, you know,

00:07:59.000 --> 00:08:00.980
tell us what to do on our own property."

00:08:01.280 --> 00:08:06.159
Building a fence also put an end to another
intrusive fan activity, which involved re-enacting

00:08:06.160 --> 00:08:10.780
a moment from a third season episode when
an angry Walter White throws a pizza on the

00:08:10.780 --> 00:08:11.280
roof.

00:08:11.720 --> 00:08:16.240
In 2015, Breaking Bad creator Vince Gilligan
implored fans to stop:

00:08:16.440 --> 00:08:22.400
"Let me tell ya, there is nothing original
or funny or cool about throwing a pizza on

00:08:22.400 --> 00:08:23.280
this lady's roof."

00:08:23.820 --> 00:08:27.380
An "Easter egg" is hidden throughout the second
season of Breaking Bad.

00:08:27.540 --> 00:08:33.380
Four non-consecutive episodes open with ominous,
black-and-white "teaser" clips, seemingly

00:08:33.380 --> 00:08:34.720
unconnected to the show.

00:08:35.020 --> 00:08:39.330
But string those four segments together, and
they form a cohesive whole, depicting the

00:08:39.330 --> 00:08:41.380
aftermath of a horrific plane crash.

00:08:41.940 --> 00:08:46.440
And when the titles of the episodes featuring
those clips are similarly placed together,

00:08:46.440 --> 00:08:52.220
in order, they form a sentence: "Seven-Thirty-Seven,"
"Down," "Over," "Albuquerque."

00:08:52.980 --> 00:08:57.520
Breaking Bad creator Vince Gilligan has said
that he and his writers "worked very hard"

00:08:57.520 --> 00:09:00.780
to come up with episode titles with "proper
dual meanings."

00:09:01.320 --> 00:09:07.590
On the surface, "Seven-Thirty Seven" refers
to the $737,000 Walter wants to leave behind

00:09:07.590 --> 00:09:08.400
for his family.

00:09:08.900 --> 00:09:11.800
"Down" is about Jesse being "down and out."

00:09:12.060 --> 00:09:16.420
"Over" concerns Walter thinking his drug-making
days are finished.

00:09:16.640 --> 00:09:21.280
And "ABQ" is an abbreviation for the show's
central location of Albuquerque.

00:09:21.460 --> 00:09:27.760
Season two wraps up with a plane crashing
practically in Walter's backyard, a 737 down

00:09:27.760 --> 00:09:28.840
over Albuquerque.

00:09:29.260 --> 00:09:30.840
Gilligan told NJ.com:

00:09:31.300 --> 00:09:36.260
"It seemed like a big showmanship moment,
and to visualize, in one fell swoop, all the

00:09:36.260 --> 00:09:40.960
terrible grief that Walt has wrought upon
his loved ones, and the community at large."

00:09:41.820 --> 00:09:45.040
The central through-line of Breaking Bad is
pretty easy to see.

00:09:45.320 --> 00:09:49.920
Walter White, once a regular family man with
a regular job as a high school teacher, slowly

00:09:49.920 --> 00:09:55.460
turns wicked as he becomes more embroiled
in the manufacture and distribution of methamphetamine.

00:09:55.860 --> 00:10:00.300
That's all very literary, but television is
a visual medium, and Breaking Bad expresses

00:10:00.300 --> 00:10:01.400
its arcs visually.

00:10:02.120 --> 00:10:07.790
In an article for TDYLF, graphic designer
John LaRue points out that the characters'

00:10:07.790 --> 00:10:11.940
trajectories can be tracked by the colors
of clothes they wear over the course of the

00:10:11.940 --> 00:10:12.440
show.

00:10:13.060 --> 00:10:18.040
Marie Schrader loves purple and wears it a
lot, but keep an eye out for the darker colors

00:10:18.040 --> 00:10:20.780
she sports during a kleptomania relapse.

00:10:21.060 --> 00:10:25.060
Walt Jr. apparently wears colors that indicate
support for other characters.

00:10:25.420 --> 00:10:29.660
Skyler White starts out favoring blue, but
her palette turns dark when she gets wise

00:10:29.660 --> 00:10:31.120
to her husband's other life.

00:10:31.560 --> 00:10:37.140
As for Walter White, he wears drab khaki after
bad cancer news or a personal setback.

00:10:37.220 --> 00:10:42.420
However, when he gets more involved with Gus
Fring, more blue shows up in his wardrobe,

00:10:42.540 --> 00:10:44.760
a nod to his signature blue meth.

00:10:45.220 --> 00:10:49.980
Of all the horrible things Walter White does
in Breaking Bad, the very worst may be letting

00:10:49.980 --> 00:10:50.680
Jane die.

00:10:51.200 --> 00:10:56.480
Portrayed by Krysten Ritter, Jane is Jesse
Pinkman's recovering addict girlfriend, whom

00:10:56.480 --> 00:10:58.700
he pulls back into the world of drugs.

00:10:59.060 --> 00:11:04.600
She dies after choking on vomit after an overdose
while Walter watches her die without doing

00:11:04.600 --> 00:11:05.940
anything to stop it.

00:11:06.040 --> 00:11:08.960
It's a dark moment, and it was almost even
darker.

00:11:09.330 --> 00:11:14.740
As part of the Tribeca Talks series, Cranston
explained that in earlier drafts of the script,

00:11:14.740 --> 00:11:16.660
Walt was even more involved in her death.

00:11:16.840 --> 00:11:17.420
He said:

00:11:17.840 --> 00:11:22.160
"She starts to cough and she's on her side,
and Walt looks at her, and pushes her shoulder

00:11:22.160 --> 00:11:24.660
so she's on her back, essentially killing
her."

00:11:24.980 --> 00:11:30.380
AMC and producer Sony Television objected
to that bit, not because it was too awful,

00:11:30.380 --> 00:11:33.760
but because it would mean Walt would "break
bad" too soon.

00:11:33.980 --> 00:11:36.540
That still represented an acting challenge
for Cranston.

00:11:37.160 --> 00:11:39.800
When Ritter shot the vomiting scene, Cranston
said that

00:11:40.100 --> 00:11:44.940
"[Ritter's face] lost all characteristics,
and out of that came the face of my real daughter

00:11:44.940 --> 00:11:45.660
choking to death."

00:11:46.260 --> 00:11:50.000
The actor added that even years later, he
still gets a little choked up about it.

00:11:50.660 --> 00:11:56.620
Breaking Bad's fifth and final season premiere
hit AMC on August 11, 2013, ending with the

00:11:56.620 --> 00:12:00.180
on-screen message, "Dedicated to our friend
Kevin Cordasco."

00:12:00.400 --> 00:12:04.660
That's the name of a Breaking Bad mega-fan
who died earlier in the year after a long

00:12:04.660 --> 00:12:05.920
fight against cancer.

00:12:06.340 --> 00:12:08.580
Kevin Cordasco Sr. told The Hollywood Reporter:

00:12:09.040 --> 00:12:12.760
"He and his friends watched it obsessively
and ate pizza in his bedroom.

00:12:13.160 --> 00:12:16.440
There was something about the Walter White
character…the way he took control of his

00:12:16.440 --> 00:12:19.420
illness, and his life, that really resonated
with Kevin."

00:12:19.790 --> 00:12:24.460
As it turns out, Cordasco's godmother set
up a cast visit, getting Bryan Cranston to

00:12:24.460 --> 00:12:26.320
come meet his biggest fan.

00:12:26.580 --> 00:12:31.000
Gilligan apparently asked Cordasco if there
was anything he'd like the show to explore.

00:12:31.320 --> 00:12:35.500
Cordasco responded that he wanted to know
more about Gretchen and Elliot, the couple

00:12:35.540 --> 00:12:39.900
who, with Walter White, formed Gray Matter
years before the show started.

00:12:39.960 --> 00:12:43.320
Thanks to Cordasco, Gretchen and Elliot reappear
in season five.

